joining me now is simon fraser who's a  former head of the foreign office  good morning simon um good morning  you're a seasoned diplomat you've seen a  lot of this um are we really facing the  biggest land war in europe as described  by the prime minister  well of course it's always difficult  disentangle the disinformation from the  from the facts but it does seem that  actually contrary to what a lot of the  experts have been predicting over recent  weeks  we may well be moving in that direction  and we do have to take very seriously  the warnings that are being issued by uh  president biden and the prime minister  and others now i think so the situation  does look pretty alarming at the moment  the um the conference is still going on  but the russians aren't there um  can there be a diplomatic solution if  they just aren't even turning up  well uh it's true that they're not there  and i think that what that tells us is  that uh president putin is not at the  moment really interested in negotiation  and a negotiated solution uh he's made  demands which he knew nato and the  americans could not accept he's kept the  door open a bit through the discussions  of the activity of his foreign minister  lavrov but it doesn't seem to me at the  moment that there is a very active  disposition to negotiate on the russian  side and i mean there are ways in which  negotiations could be taken forward uh  if both sides wanted to do it but one  thing's really important to say we can't  negotiate with russia if russia has  broken international law has infringed  the sovereignty of ukraine and has  invaded so the window for diplomacy at  the moment the current window for  diplomacy does look as if it may be  closing  taking all of that together do you agree  with the europe minister james cleverley  when he says as he did earlier in this  program  that uh an invasion is more likely today  than not likely  i just don't know the answer to that i  mean if you  you know if you look at this rationally  from our perspective you would say that  the risks for putin of doing that are  immense  and he's going to incur huge economic  political  military and human cost uh if he does  that  but what we have to be always aware of  is that other people don't always see  the world through the same perspective  as us and we have to understand the  different motivators that are driving uh  president putin to this action uh even  if we don't agree with them we have to  try and understand them i can't tell you  exactly what's going on in his head and  what he's going to do i think we do have  to take the risk seriously  well what's going on his head as you say  uh is a bit of a a mystery but um the  foreign secretary this morning appears  to  have  better intelligence she says that putin  won't stop at ukraine and that he has  designed on the baltics and elsewhere  um  if she's right that means that this  crisis  has to escalate and at some point there  is going to be  actual conflict  yes i think what he's trying to do is a  number of things he's trying to do he's  basically trying to rewrite  the sort of european security structures  that we've had since the cold war which  he believes have been shameful for  russia a reverse for russia he's trying  to reintegrate ukraine into russia  because that he believes is something he  believed he wrote a long essay on that  recently and it's something he believes  in  he's trying to weaken america and divide  the west and he's trying of course to  bolster  russian uh control through force in the  near abroad all those are recognized  objectives of russian policy and what  he's trying to do actually is exploit a  moment of what he sees to be weakness in  the west and distraction in the west in  america and in europe to achieve those  goals so there is a risk that he will  act in ukraine and of course we must be  aware that he may have broader uh  objectives that's why deterring him from  this action would have been uh 